-- title:
--   Exactly n level automorphisms stabilise the exceptional valuation, q=3
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ denotes `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, and for a subgroup $H\le(\mathbb{Z}/N_0)^\times$ and a field $L$ of characteristic zero a *level automorphism attached to $\gamma\in SL_2(\mathbb{Z})$* means the predicate [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ξ q N₀ H γ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29): for every weight $k$, every pair $f,g$ of modular forms of weight $k$ on [`CohCarrier.GammaH N₀ H`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) (viewed inside $GL_2(\mathbb{R})$), every pair $p_f,p_g$ of power series over $\mathbb{Z}$ that are integral $q$-expansions of $f$ and of $g$ with the image of $p_g$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ nonzero, every $x\in K$ whose Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of the quotient of these two series, and every ring homomorphism $\iota:L\to\mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\xi)=\exp(2\pi i/q)$, the identity
--   $$\iota_*\bigl(\tau x\bigr)\cdot\bigl(\text{$q$-expansion of }g\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma\bigr)=\bigl(\text{$q$-expansion of }f\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma\bigr)$$
--   holds, where $\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma=\begin{pmatrix}a&b/q\\ qc&d\end{pmatrix}$ for $\gamma=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\ c&d\end{pmatrix}$. Routine typeclass assumptions are not listed separately.
--
--   **Arithmetic and reduction data.** A prime $q$ with $q=3$; a nonzero $M'$ with $q\nmid M'$; a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g\equiv 11\pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g\mid M'$. A valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A$. A finite set $W$ of places of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` (the subfield of Laurent series over the residue field of $A$ generated by the expansions of $j$ and of $j(\mathsf q^{M'})$) whose members are, by `hW`, exactly the places in `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`: those places $w$ that are rational, satisfy `IsAffineGeomPlace`, and have $w(\mathrm{jGeomGen})\in$ `ssJSet q`, the set of $j$ such that every elliptic curve with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point; and a chosen $s\in W$. The hypothesis `hle` states the inclusion `modularFunctionFieldBar M' ≤ fieldBar q M'` of the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the level-$M'$ modular function field (generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the expansions $j(\mathsf q^{d})$, $d\mid M'$) into the base change of the function field of $X_{H}(q^2M')$ for $H=$ `levelH q M'`, the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$. Furthermore $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` to `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'`, that is: a valuation subring `R₀.integers` of the source, a surjective ring homomorphism `R₀.residue` onto the target with kernel the maximal ideal, a map on places preserving degrees, compatibility with $A$ and with $\mathrm{res}_A$, the scaling property and the order-compatibility of the place map. The hypothesis `hR₀` states that for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that element lies in `R₀.integers` and its `R₀`-residue, read as a Laurent series over the residue field of $A$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   **Base field and constants.** An intermediate field $k_0$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$ and $\pi_0\in k_0$ lying in $A$; writing $A\cap k_0$ for the comap of $A$ along $k_0\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` say that $A\cap k_0$ is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, henselian local, with algebraically closed residue field, and `hκ` says every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0$ lying in $A$. A prime $\ell$ with $3\le\ell$, $\ell\ne q$, $\ell\nmid M'$; an element $\zeta_0\in k_0$ that is a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity; an element $\varpi_t\in k_0$ lying in $A$ such that $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=q\cdot u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$. An intermediate field $K_b$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/k_0$ with $K_b=\bot$, a valuation subring $A_b$ of $K_b$ cut out by membership in $A$, and a generator $\varpi_b\ne 0$ of its maximal ideal. The tameness hypothesis `htame` states that $\ell_g-1$ is a unit of $A\cap k_0$, and $\xi\in k_0$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity.
--
--   **Level and function field.** $H_1$ is the intersection of `levelH q M'` with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb{Z}/\ell_g)^\times$, so $H_1$ consists of the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ and modulo $\ell_g$; $K_\ell$ is the intermediate field `laurentBaseChange k₀ (xHFunctionField (q^2*M') H₁)` of the Laurent series over $k_0$, an algebra over $A\cap k_0$ compatibly with $k_0$; $j_\ell\in K_\ell$ has Laurent series the coefficientwise image of `jq` and is nonzero; and $\varpi_t'\in A\cap k_0$ maps to $\varpi_t$.
--
--   **The supersingular point of the chart.** $y$ is a maximal ideal of `chartAlgFin (A∩k₀) Kℓ jℓ`, the subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $(A\cap k_0)[j_\ell]$; `hϖy` says $\pi_0\in y$; `hss` says that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the chart algebra with kernel $y$, $\varphi(j_\ell)$ lies in `ssJSet q Ω`. The hypothesis `hover` identifies $y$ as a point over $s$: for every $g$ in the level-$M'$ modular function field over $\mathbb{Q}$ whose base change lies in `R₀.integers`, having non-negative order at every place of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` at which $j$ has non-negative order, and whose `R₀`-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and for every witness that the coefficientwise image of $g$ lies in $K_\ell$ and in the chart algebra, and for every $c\in k_0$ lying in $A$ whose residue in the residue field of $A$ equals the value at $s$ of the `R₀`-residue of $g$, the difference of the chart element of $g$ and the constant $c$ lies in $y$. The hypothesis `hOrbit` says that every maximal ideal $y'$ of the chart algebra containing $\pi_0$ and satisfying the same condition over $s$ is reached from $y$: there exist $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and a $k_0$-automorphism $\tau$ of $K_\ell$ that is a level automorphism attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ (for the data $q,\xi,q,q^2M',H_1$), preserving the chart algebra, with $f\in y\iff\tau f\in y'$ for chart elements $f$.
--
--   **The rigid chart $B_t$ and the exceptional valuation $W_t$.** $B_t$ is a subalgebra of $K_\ell$ over $A\cap k_0$ and $W_t$ a valuation subring of $K_\ell$ with $B_t\subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`). The hypothesis `hS1` (fourteen clauses, summarised here) requires: the chart algebra is contained in $B_t$ and $K_\ell$ is the fraction field of $B_t$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A\cap k_0$ and $B_t/(\pi_0)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; $W_t\cap k_0=A\cap k_0$, the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is generated by $\pi_0$, $W_t$ is a discrete valuation ring, its centre on the chart algebra is exactly $y$, and $W_t$ is the localisation of $B_t$ at its maximal ideal; a Drinfeld-component clause, namely that for every algebra structure of `GaloisField q 2` on the residue field $\kappa$ of $A\cap k_0$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho:B_t\to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q κ`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) (the quotient of $\kappa[X_0,X_1]$ by the ideal generated by `drinfeldPoly q κ` $-1$) whose vanishing locus on $B_t$ is the maximal ideal of $W_t$, which is compatible with reduction of constants, and which is equivariant in the following linked form: for every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and every level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ with $f\in W_t\iff\tau f\in W_t$ there is a unit $c$ of `GaloisField q 2` with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma,c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) (the kernel of the character $(g,c)\mapsto\det(g)\,c^{q+1}$) such that $\rho(\tau b)=\mathrm{hAction}(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma,c)(\rho b)$ for all $b\in B_t$ with $\tau b\in B_t$, such that $c\ne 1$ whenever $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity, and such that the same scalar $c$ is realised with trivial matrix part, by some $\gamma'\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphism $\tau'$ attached to $\gamma'^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ with $(1,c)\in$ `hSubgroup q` and $\rho(\tau' b)=\mathrm{hAction}(1,c)(\rho b)$; finally, $B_t$ is stable under all level automorphisms attached to elements of $\Gamma_0(M')$, every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ is hit by the image of the maximal ideal of $W_t$ under some level automorphism coming from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, and an element of $B_t$ all of whose $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$-translates lie in the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is divisible by $\pi_0$.
--
--   **The generator $\tau_0$, the crossing data and the ends.** A natural number $n$ with $1\le n$ and $n\mid q+1$; an element $\gamma_0\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ together with a level automorphism $\tau_0$ attached to $\gamma_0^{-1}$ satisfying $f\in W_t\iff\tau_0 f\in W_t$; a natural number $m\ge1$ with $\pi_0^m=\varpi_t'\cdot w$ for some unit $w$ of $A\cap k_0$; an element $\zeta_c$ of the adic completion of $A\cap k_0$ with $\zeta_c^n=1$ and $\zeta_c^k-1$ a unit for $0<k<n$; and a finite set `ends` of subrings of $K_\ell$. The hypothesis `hR7` (ten clauses, summarised here) requires: $\tau_0^{\,n}$ fixes $B_t$ pointwise while $\tau_0^{\,k}$ moves some element of $B_t$ for $0<k<n$, and every level automorphism attached to some $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with a power $\tau_0^{\,k}$, $k<n$; `ends` has $q+1$ elements, is permuted by the level automorphisms attached to elements of $\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ and transitively so, each of its members is stabilised by $\tau_0$, and any two distinct members are separated by an element that is a non-unit in the one and a unit in the other; each end $O$ is contained in $W_t$, is local and noetherian, contains the chart algebra, meets $k_0$ in exactly $A\cap k_0$, and has every element congruent to a constant modulo non-units, and carries a crossing description: $\pi_0\in O$, elements $c_x,c_y\in O$, a unit $u$ of $O$, an isomorphism $\iota$ of the adic completion of $O$ with the crossing model `UVCrossingModel` of the completion of $A\cap k_0$ at $\pi_0^m$ (that is, $\widehat{A\cap k_0}[[U,V]]/(UV-\pi_0^m)$, assumed local), units $\gamma_U,\gamma_V$, an automorphism $\theta_0$ of the crossing model over the completed base and $\zeta_c'$ inverse to $\zeta_c$, such that $c_xc_y=\pi_0^m u$, $\iota$ takes constants to constants, $\iota(c_x)=\gamma_U U$ and $\iota(c_y)=\gamma_V V$, $c_y$ lies in and $c_x$ lies outside the maximal ideal of $W_t$, $\iota$ transports the action of $\tau_0$ on $O$ into $\theta_0$, and $\theta_0(U)-\zeta_c U$ and $\theta_0(V)-\zeta_c' V$ lie in the square of the maximal ideal; together with, for each end, an auxiliary valuation subring $W_x$ of $K_\ell$ containing $O$ and the chart algebra, meeting $k_0$ in $A\cap k_0$, a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, admitting an element $t$ such that any polynomial over $A\cap k_0$ whose value at $t$ lies in the maximal ideal of $W_x$ has all coefficients in the maximal ideal of $A\cap k_0$, with $c_x$ in and $c_y$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_x$, whose centre on the chart algebra is contained in $y$ but differs from it; and a subalgebra $B_x$ of $K_\ell$ over $A\cap k_0$, contained in $B_t\cap O$, of finite type, stable under the level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, consisting of elements regular at every place of $K_\ell/k_0$ at which $j_\ell$ is regular, and with $O$ the localisation of $B_x$ at the elements invertible in $O$. The last clause of `hR7` is a dichotomy: every valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ meeting $k_0$ in $A\cap k_0$, containing the chart algebra and with every chart element of $y$ in the maximal ideal of $V$, either contains $B_t$ or contains some end $O\in$ `ends` with every non-unit of $O$ lying in the maximal ideal of $V$.
--
--   Finally, the hypothesis `hR5d` states that for every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and every level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$, if $\tau$ stabilises $y$ on the chart algebra (for chart elements $b$ with $\tau b$ again in the chart, $b\in y\iff\tau b\in y$), then $f\in W_t\iff\tau f\in W_t$ for all $f\in K_\ell$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** The set of $k_0$-algebra automorphisms $\tau$ of $K_\ell$ such that ($\tau$ is a level automorphism attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ for some $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, for the data $q,\xi,q,q^2M',H_1$) and such that $f\in W_t\iff\tau f\in W_t$ for all $f\in K_\ell$ has cardinality exactly $n$.
--
--   This is the counting step for the decomposition group at a supersingular point of the regular model: it says that the level automorphisms coming from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ which stabilise the exceptional valuation $W_t$ centred at $y$ form a set of exactly $n$ elements, $n$ being the exact order of the generator $\tau_0$ on the rigid chart $B_t$. It is the $q=3$, $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-level form of that count, with the scalar clause carried inside the Drinfeld-component clause of `hS1`, and it feeds the identification of the decomposition order with the number of rational automorphisms at the corresponding moduli place.
